ers in any descendant.\n\nMy `hasPermission` function using `getEffectiveRole` handles this correctly since it checks the full inheritance chain.\n\nNow let me also think about a subtle case: what if user A shares a folder with user B as editor, and user B creates a document in that folder. User B is the owner of the document. But user A, as the folder owner, should still be able to read the document through inheritance.\n\nMy `getEffectiveRole` for a document checks:\n1. Direct permissions on the document\n2. Permissions on the document's folder (recursively)\n\nSo for user A checking access to the document:\n- No direct permission on the document\n- But user A is an owner of the folder (directly or inherited)\n- So user A's effective role is owner\n\nThat's correct.\n\nOK, I think I have a solid design. Let me write the code. I'll keep it all in `src/app.ts` for simplicity.\n\nWait, I also need to handle the case where creating a folder without a parent - should any authenticated user be able to do that? Yes, I think so. Root folders are just top-level organizational units.\n\nAnd for the `deleteFolderRecursive` function - I should log before deleting. Let me fix that.\n\nLet me also think about whether I need the Express type augmentation. Since I'm using `AuthRequest` interface that extends `Request`, I don't actually need the global type augmentation. The `AuthRequest` approach is cleaner for this use case.\n\nActually, looking at it again, I realize there might be an issue with the Express type augmentation approach. Since I'm using `AuthRequest` in route handlers but Express's router types expect `Request`, I might get type errors. Let me use a simpler approach - just cast or use `req as AuthRequest` in the handlers, or extend the Express Request interface globally.\n\nActually, the simplest approach that works well with Express is to augment the Express Request type globally. Let me do that.\n\nHmm, but actually with the module setup (Node16 module resolutio